{"kind":"request","text":"claude: spike one of three from the JSONata/DDL implementation note — the historical view and atomic frontier recovery. Hugh has authorised progressing all three spikes and then the chess replatform.\n\nWHAT THE NOTE ASKS FOR, quoted so nothing softens in transit: prove atomic frontier recovery after a simulated crash, alongside the historical view.\n\nWHY YOU AND NOT CODEX. Codex has the query-sandbox spike, filed at 466a084e, and these two are the pair the note's stop rule names together — failure of either is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface. Running them in parallel under different actors gets both answers sooner and keeps each judgement independent, which matters when the outcome may kill the driver choice. They touch the same spike tree, so coordinate on paths rather than both editing spike/jsonataddl; if that turns out to be unworkable, say so and I will sequence them instead of parallelising.\n\nWHAT I WANT BEYOND A PASS. The interesting result is not that recovery works on a clean crash, but where it stops working. A simulated crash that always happens at a convenient point proves less than one that happens at an inconvenient one. Say which interruption points you tested and which you could not — mid-transaction, after write before commit, after commit before frontier advance, during checkpoint — and say plainly if any of those is untestable with the current driver, because that is a design fact rather than a testing gap.\n\nIf recovery is not atomic in some case, report the case. Do not narrow the claim to the cases that pass; the note exists to find this out, and a spike that only confirms what we hoped has not earned its cost.\n\nTHE NOTE'S BOUNDARY IS A CONDITION. Failure here is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface. It is not grounds to add database concepts to the sequencing kernel or enlarge the minimal application. If the fix you want requires the kernel to learn about the database, stop and file that as a finding rather than doing it.\n\nSCOPE. A spike under spike/, not productionisation; codex's closure report bd203d4c put that explicitly out of scope and hugh has not changed it. Sequenced after your current lanes — the three decision-records repairs and the cloned-repo review — and after you retire the three superseded proposal originals I flagged at 5438ca4b, which is a two-minute job that is currently making hugh's ratification queue lie.","body":{"conditions":"Run the historical-view and atomic-frontier-recovery spike from notes/2026-08-26-jsonata-ddl-application-implementation.md under spike/, without productionising anything. Prove atomic frontier recovery after a simulated crash, and exercise the historical view. Enumerate the interruption points you tested — at minimum mid-transaction, after write before commit, after commit before frontier advance, and during checkpoint — and state for each whether recovery is atomic, non-atomic, or untestable with the current driver; an untestable point is a design fact to report, not a gap to pass over. Where recovery is not atomic, report the case rather than narrowing the claim to the cases that pass. Tests must be mutation-sensitive: break the recovery path, watch the named assertion fail, restore, and confirm the tree is clean. Coordinate paths with codex's query-sandbox spike 466a084e so you are not both editing the same files; if parallel work on the spike tree proves unworkable, report that and planner will sequence instead. The note's boundary is a condition: failure is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface, and is not grounds to add database concepts to the sequencing kernel or enlarge the minimal application — if the fix requires the kernel to learn about the database, stop and file it as a finding. Exact clean pushed head, gofmt, build, vet, git diff --check and go test ./... -count=1 green, per-path artifacts at every changed stable path resting on your promise plus stable bases, and independent review recording Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not merge. Sequenced after your decision-records repairs, the cloned-repo review, and the retirement of the three superseded proposal originals.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}